Hamilton 8 is Durham's internal supercomputer.
It is a system powered by AMD EPYC processors.
I nevertheless prefer the Intel toolchain on the machine.


# Loading Intel's toolchain

Hamilton's default oneapi module is built against a GCC version which is too old and lacks
important features within its standard library.
Therefore, you have to manuall load the latest gcc.
This is usually prevented by the system and leads to a conflict (so that you
can't have multiple GCC versions loaded).
We therefore have to disable conflict checks a priori.
Eventually, I end up with 

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module purge
module load oneapi/2025.2
export FLAVOUR_NOCONFLICT=1
module load gcc/15.2

module load tbb/2022.0
 
module load intelmpi/2021.16
export I_MPI_CXX=icpx

module load python/3.13.9
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

For the vectorisation, we recognise that we always need

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 -O3 
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The previously used ```-Ofast``` is deprecated now.
Also ```-ffast-math``` seems not to work anymore.



# OpenMP configurations

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
./configure CXX=icpx CC=icx CXXFLAGS="-std=c++20 -O3 -mtune=native -march=native -fma -fomit-frame-pointer -fno-exceptions -Wno-unknown-attributes -Wno-gcc-compat -fiopenmp" LIBS=-fiopenmp  --enable-mghype --enable-exahype --enable-blockstructured --enable-finiteelements --with-multithreading=omp --enable-loadbalancing
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

No surprises here. 
We usually use the Intel OpenMP runtime, which is enabled through ```-fiopenmp``` as compared to the generic LLVM OpenMP runtime.

<div style="background-color: #fcc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
  Peano's core code does not use exceptions and the ```-fno-exceptions``` flag hence works.
  However, some external libraries such as those used by ExaGRyPE do use 
  exceptions, and you therefore have to remove this flag from the configuration. 
</div>


# TBB configurations

We offer these two TBB backends: One with our own extensions, which kind of
serve as preview of upcoming TBB variants, and then we have an implementation
which uses these new features yet requires a relatively new TBB version in 
return. 
@ref tbb_extension "There is a separate page" discussing these particular 
features. It also describes how to switch them on partially. For Hamilton,
we usually use the old-fashioned way:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module load tbb/2022.2

./configure CXX=icpx CC=icx CXXFLAGS="-std=c++20 -O3 -mtune=native -march=native -fma -fomit-frame-pointer -fno-exceptions -Wno-unknown-attributes -Wno-gcc-compat" LIBS="-ltbb" LDFLAGS="-L${TBBROOT}/lib" --enable-mghype --enable-exahype --enable-blockstructured --enable-finiteelements --with-multithreading=tbb_extension --enable-loadbalancing
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

There seems to be an issue with the libraries (at least was up to TBB 2024.2 on all systems that we tried).
So we better set the library path explicitly.

<div style="background-color: #fcc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
  Peano's core code does not use exceptions and the ```-fno-exceptions``` flag hence works.
  However, some external libraries such as those used by ExaGRyPE do use 
  exceptions, and you therefore have to remove this flag from the configuration. 
</div>

# Intel MPI

If you use mpiicpc, the MPI wrapper still refers to icpc even though icpc is
officially deprecated.
Therefore, I manually have to repoint it to icpx.
The other options to do this (via additional script arguments, e.g.) all failed
on this machine.



~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
./configure CXX=icpx CC=icx CXXFLAGS=-O3 -ffast-math -mtune=native -march=native -fma -fomit-frame-pointer -std=c++20 -fno-exceptions -Wno-unknown-attributes -Wno-gcc-compat LIBS=-ltbb LDFLAGS=-L/apps/developers/libraries/tbb/2022.0/1/default/tbb/latest/lib --enable-mghype --enable-exahype --enable-blockstructured --enable-finiteelements --with-multithreading=tbb_extension --enable-loadbalancing --with-mpi=mpiicpc
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


## Older settings (not tested atm)

- MPI production runs
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
./configure CC=icpx CXX=icpx CXXFLAGS="-Ofast -g -std=c++20 -mtune=native -march=native -fma -fomit-frame-pointer -qopenmp -Wno-unknown-attributes" LDFLAGS="-fiopenmp -g" --with-multithreading=omp --enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured --enable-particles --with-mpi=mpiicpc FC=gfortran
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Performance analysis runs with single node tracing
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module load vtune
./configure CC=icpx CXX=icpx CXXFLAGS="-Ofast -g -std=c++20 -mtune=native -march=native -fma -fomit-frame-pointer -qopenmp -Wno-unknown-attributes -I${VTUNE_HOME}/vtune/latest/include" LDFLAGS="-qopenmp -g -L${VTUNE_HOME}/vtune/latest/lib64" LIBS="-littnotify" --with-multithreading=omp --enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured --enable-particles --with-mpi=mpiicpc FC=gfortran --with-toolchain=itt
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- MPI performance analysis runs
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module load vtune
./configure CC=icpx CXX=icpx CXXFLAGS="-I${VTUNE_HOME}/vtune/latest/include -I${VT_ROOT}/include -Ofast -g -std=c++20 -mtune=native -march=native -fma -fomit-frame-pointer -qopenmp -Wno-unknown-attributes" LDFLAGS="-qopenmp -g -L${VT_LIB_DIR} -L${VTUNE_HOME}/vtune/latest/lib64" LIBS="-lVT ${VT_ADD_LIBS} -littnotify" --with-multithreading=omp --enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured --enable-particles --with-mpi=mpiicpc FC=gfortran --with-toolchain=itac
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


Further to these flags, I @ref page_faq_home "pause and resume the data collection" manually in
ExaHyPE and Swift. This is however something I do in the main() routine of
the respective applications.


